      .sub-content
        %h1= st_h1
        %p.sub-lead= st_lead

        %h2= st_h2_lineage
        %p
          <strong>Ondas is not a PteronautOS invention.</strong> The wave-modulation family —
          breath-pause cadence control, stroke-shape ferocity, asymmetric dwell, and the
          aeroelastic coupling of wing load to PID authority — lives and evolves in the
          <a href="https://dantiel.github.io/OrniFlight/" target="_blank">OrniFlight</a>
          project, whose wiki and simulator carry the advanced algorithms (including the
          <a href="https://dantiel.github.io/OrniFlight/en/wiki/servo_efficiency.html" target="_blank">servo-efficiency table</a>).
        %p
          PteronautOS embeds a <strong>partially simplified implementation</strong>: the same
          mathematical breath geometry, scaled to an ESP8285 with one MPU6050 and up to nine
          servos. Where OrniFlight simulates continuous aeroelastics and explores whole envelope
          families offline, PteronautOS must decide <em>in 4 ms ticks</em>, on an 80 MHz core,
          with the ExpressLRS radio stack sharing the same silicon. Every gain in this article
          exists in both projects; the firmware constants below are the airborne subset.

        %h2= st_h2_pipeline
        %p
          The bridge from the gyro to the wingbeat is deliberately short — four files, no
          dynamic allocation, no queues:
        %pre
          %code
            :preserve
              MPU6050 raw (I2C, 1 kHz register reads)
                ▼
              Zephyrus::update()            Mahony AHRS → roll°, pitch°, yawRate °/s
                ▼                            dual PID: roll→0°, pitch→0°, yawRate→0°/s
              4 raw pitch terms exposed      pitchPTerm · pitchITerm · pitchDTerm · pitchErrorRate
                ▼
              ZephyrusFilter.h (250 Hz)      copies terms onto ornithopter.gyroPitch*
                ▼
              Ornithopter::_computeServoMixer() / _computeGearboxMixer()
                ▼   Ondas P → phase · PD → dwell · I → asymmetry · SSFF → next stroke
              _f[]  →  funcMap → wing / crest-rudder / tail servos (µs)
        %p
          The four raw terms are written in <code>Zephyrus::update()</code> immediately after the
          pitch PID computes its correction:
        %pre
          %code
            :preserve
              pitchPTerm     = ZEPHYR_PID_PITCH_KP * pitchErr;                 // raw proportional
              pitchITerm     = _pidPitch.integrator;                           // accumulated error
              pitchDTerm     = ZEPHYR_PID_PITCH_KD * _pidPitch.lastDerivative; // low-pass filtered D
              pitchErrorRate = _pidPitch.lastDerivative;                       // °/s, for SSFF
        %p
          All four are guarded by a NaN trap (if any input is NaN, all four are zeroed) so a
          corrupted I²C frame can never reach the mixer arithmetic. Seven fields on
          <code>Ornithopter</code> receive the bridge data:

        %table
          %tr
            %th Field
            %th Zéfiro source
            %th Consumed by
          %tr
            %td <code>gyroPitchPTerm</code>
            %td <code>pitchPTerm</code>
            %td Ondas P → phase advance (cadence channel)
          %tr
            %td <code>gyroPitchITerm</code>
            %td <code>pitchITerm</code>
            %td Ondas I → stroke asymmetry (balance channel)
          %tr
            %td <code>gyroPitchDTerm</code>
            %td <code>pitchDTerm</code>
            %td Ondas D → dwell ratio (ferocity channel)
          %tr
            %td <code>gyroPitchErrorRate</code>
            %td <code>pitchErrorRate</code>
            %td SSFF half-stroke accumulation
          %tr
            %td <code>gyroRudderCorrection</code>
            %td <code>rudderCorrection</code>
            %td Crest rudder µs offset (servo &amp; gearbox kernels)
          %tr
            %td <code>gyroAileronCorrection</code>
            %td <code>rollCorrection</code> × gearbox gain
            %td Gearbox: roll PID → V-tail aileron surfaces
          %tr
            %td <code>gyroElevatorCorrection</code>
            %td <code>pitchCorrection</code> × gain × <code>aeroGainScale</code>
            %td Gearbox: pitch PID → elevon / elevator surfaces

        %h2= st_h2_ondas
        %p
          <em>Ondas</em> (Portuguese: "waves") is the three-channel breath system that maps the
          pitch PID's raw P, I and D terms onto three orthogonal dimensions of the flapping
          waveform. It is a <strong>proprioceptive loop</strong>: the gyro's sense of pitch
          directly shapes the wing motion, and the wing motion changes the pitch the gyro feels.
        %h3 Cadence — P → phase advance
        %p
          A positive pitch error (nose up) advances the oscillator phase, shortening the current
          half-stroke; a negative error retards it. The ideal law scales the oscillator spring:
        %pre
          %code
            :preserve
              _osc.kGainMod = 1.0f + gyroPitchPTerm * aeroGainScale * cadenceGain * 0.00005f;
              // clamped to [0.5, 2.0] — phase can at most double or halve
        %p
          The oscillator <code>advance()</code> consumes it as
          <code>error = kGain · kGainMod · cadenceTarget − kDamp · cadence</code>. Tuning range
          0–100, default 20.
        %h3 Ferocity — PD blend into dwell
        %p
          The P and D terms are blended into a single ferocity signal that sharpens or softens
          the waveform's dwell:
        %pre
          %code
            :preserve
              ferocitySignal = (gyroPitchPTerm * ferocityPGain * 0.00015f
                               + gyroPitchDTerm * ferocityDGain * 0.0003f) * aeroGainScale;
              // clamped to [-0.5, 0.5], added to both stroke and return ferocity
        %p
          With <code>ferocityPGain = 0</code> (the default) the behaviour is D-only — rapid pitch
          changes produce sharper strokes while steady flight stays sinusoidal.
        %h3 Balance — I into asymmetry
        %p
          The integral term shifts the stroke centre: a persistent nose-up bias strengthens the
          downstroke, a persistent nose-down bias strengthens the upstroke.
        %pre
          %code
            :preserve
              float iBias = gyroPitchITerm * aeroGainScale * balanceGain * 0.0001f;
              // clamped to [-3.0, 3.0]; added to strokeFer, subtracted from returnFer
        %p
          Balance is the most destabilising channel when set high — the ideal tuning guide keeps
          it low (5–15) and adds it last. Ferocity application, combining pilot command, Ondas
          and SSFF biases, keeps the pilot's authority over the full <code>[1.0, 8.0]</code>
          window:
        %pre
          %code
            :preserve
              strokeFer = pilot(stroke) + ferocitySignal + iBias + _ssffFerocityUpBias
              returnFer = pilot(return) + ferocitySignal − iBias + _ssffFerocityDownBias

        %h2= st_h2_shape
        %p
          The waveform itself follows the OrniFlight stroke family: each half uses its own
          ferocity, a shared reversal threshold keeps left/right wings in phase, and a
          per-profile <em>shape mix</em> continuously transmutes a flat dwell/plateau stroke into
          a rounded pyramidal path. The interactive explorer below renders the commanded
          geometry for any down/up ferocity pair — the same curve the mixer requests each tick.
        %p= doc_wave_p1
        %p= doc_wave_p2

        .waveform-explorer{"data-ferocity-explorer": true, "data-i18n-lock-sync": doc_lock_sync, "data-i18n-lock-oppose": doc_lock_oppose, "data-i18n-lock-unlocked": doc_lock_unlocked, "data-i18n-axis-wing": doc_axis_wing, "data-i18n-axis-cycle": doc_axis_cycle, "data-i18n-axis-half": doc_axis_half, "data-i18n-metric": doc_metric}
