          %strong= doc_caution_title
          = doc_caution_body
        %h3= doc_read_h3
        %p= doc_read_p

        %h2= st_h2_anchor
        %p
          The oscillator's damping is exposed as a tunable anchor. The base law is a damped
          spring toward the cadence target:
        %pre
          %code
            :preserve
              kDamp = 10.0f + anchorGain;                  // anchorGain 0 → k₂=10 (tight)
              error = kGain * kGainMod * cadenceTarget
                    - kDamp * cadence;
        %p
          At <code>anchorGain = 0</code> (default) damping is tight — rapid convergence to the
          commanded cadence. Higher values raise k₂ toward 110 at gain 100, locking the rhythm
          harder and rejecting perturbations. Tuning range 0–100.

        %h2= st_h2_resonance
        %p
          Resonance is a phase-locked lock-in amplifier: it multiplies the pitch error rate by
          <code>sin(oscillator phase)</code> and accumulates — reinforcing the stroke exactly
          when a correction is most effective, damping it when it would fight the wing.
        %pre
          %code
            :preserve
              _resonanceAccum += gyroPitchErrorRate * sinf(_osc.phase)
                               * resonanceGain * 0.01f * dt;
              _resonanceAccum *= expf(-dt / 0.15f);        // leaky integrator, τ = 0.15 s
              clamp(_resonanceAccum, -2.0f, 2.0f);
        %p
          The accumulated value is injected into both stroke and return ferocity for the next
          frame. At <code>resonanceGain = 0</code> (default) the whole path is disabled. The
          leaky decay prevents runaway accumulation during sustained disturbance.

        %h2= st_h2_ssff
        %p
          Stroke-synchronous feed-forward is philosophically different from every PID term:
          it corrects the <em>next</em> half-stroke from what the gyro measured during the
          <em>current</em> one. At each zero-crossing of the flapping sine (wing reversal), the
          mean pitch error rate of the finished half-stroke becomes a bias on the opposite
          half's ferocity.
        %pre
          %code
            :preserve
              // zero-cross detected: finished one half-stroke, starting the next
              meanError = _ssffAccumError / count;
              bias      = meanError * ssffGain * 0.00001f;   // clamped ±2.0
              // upstroke just ended        → bias feeds the downstroke (power stroke)
              // downstroke just ended      → bias feeds the upstroke (recovery)
        %p
          Up-bias is applied to the downstroke (correcting for error accumulated on the
          preceding upstroke); down-bias to the upstroke. When <code>ssffGain</code> drops to
          zero the biases are explicitly cleared so a WebUI disable never leaves stale values in
          the waveform. The accumulator is 32-bit and reset per half-stroke — at 250 Hz it
          would need ~24 days of continuous flapping to overflow, which is not a practical
          concern.

        %h2= st_h2_aero
        %p
          All Ondas and SSFF modulation is scaled by a coefficient that knows whether the
          ornithopter is <em>flapping</em> or <em>gliding</em> — aeroelastic gain modulation.
          In the servo (waveform) kernel the selector is the flapping gate with its 50 µs
          hysteresis:
        %pre
          %code
            :preserve
              aeroGainScale = isFlapping ? (aeroFlapCoeff  * 0.01f)
                                         : (aeroGlideCoeff * 0.01f);
        %p
          In the gearbox kernel the selector is the motor state
          (<code>armed &amp;&amp; throttleNorm &gt; 0.1f</code>). Defaults:
        %table
          %tr
            %th Coefficient
            %th Default
            %th Range
            %th When active
          %tr
            %td <code>aeroGlideCoeff</code>
            %td 40
            %td 0–100
            %td Wings still — glide, or gearbox motor off
          %tr
            %td <code>aeroFlapCoeff</code>
            %td 40
            %td 0–100
            %td Wings flapping — or gearbox motor running
        %p
          Scaling to 0–1 makes the 0–100 slider the <em>authority</em> of the gyro over the
          wing: 40 % is a respectful copilot, 100 % is the gyro flying the waveform.

        %h2= st_h2_gearbox
        %p
          The gearbox kernel applies Zéfiro differently — there is no wing waveform to breathe,
          so the P/I/D terms modulate <em>surfaces</em> instead:
        %table
          %tr
            %th Authority
            %th Servo (waveform) kernel
            %th Gearbox kernel
          %tr
            %td Crest rudder
            %td roll + yaw combined
            %td yaw only (roll/pitch go to legs)
          %tr
            %td Wing waveform
            %td Full Ondas / SSFF breath modulation
            %td —
          %tr
            %td Leg ailerons (V-tail)
            %td —
            %td roll PID → aileron side of elevon mix
          %tr
            %td Leg elevons / elevator
            %td —
            %td pitch PID × <code>aeroGainScale</code> → elevator side
        %p
          Gearbox PID corrections are clamped to ±250 µs (<code>ZEPHYR_GEARBOX_CLAMP_US</code>)
          before mixing; the crest-rudder path is clamped to ±200 µs. One frame of lag is
          inherent: <code>aeroGainScale</code> is computed in the gearbox mixer but consumed by
          the <em>next</em> <code>zephyrusUpdate()</code> — 4 ms at 250 Hz, negligible for
          mechanical servos.

        %h2= st_h2_webui
        %p
          The ornithopter panel shows two stabilization groups when the build includes Zéfiro.
          Ondas &amp; SSFF sliders (0–100): Cadence P→Phase, Ferocity D→Dwell, Ferocity P→Dwell,
          Balance I→Asymmetry, Anchor k₂, Resonance, SSFF Gain. Aeroelastic group: Glide
          Coefficient and Flap Coefficient. All values persist through
          <code>POST /pteronautos/config</code> and stream back through
          <code>GET /pteronautos/state</code>:
        %pre
          %code
            :preserve
              {
                "ornithopter": {
                  "cadence_gain": 20.0, "ferocity_d_gain": 20.0, "balance_gain": 10.0,
                  "ferocity_p_gain": 0.0, "anchor_gain": 0.0, "resonance_gain": 0.0,
                  "ssff_gain": 0.0,
                  "aero_glide_coeff": 40.0, "aero_flap_coeff": 40.0,
                  "aero_gain_scale": 0.4
                }
              }
        %p
          <code>aero_gain_scale</code> is computed and read-only. POST accepts partial updates and
          clamps every field to <code>[0, 100]</code> server-side.

        %h2= st_h2_tuning
        %p
          The ideal flight-test sequence — each step is a flight, not a slider drag:
        %ol
          %li
            <strong>Zero every gain.</strong> Fly manual only; the airframe must be stable and
            predictable before the gyro earns a vote.
          %li
            <strong>Enable Zéfiro's crest rudder</strong> (roll+yaw PID). Verify the rudder
            resists a gentle wing-rock before anything touches the waveform.
          %li
            <strong>Cadence (P→Phase)</strong> from 10 upward. Too high = jerky wings, phase
            hunting; too low = nothing. Right = wings feel "connected" to pitch.
          %li
            <strong>Ferocity D (D→Dwell)</strong> from 10. Too high = the waveform oscillates
            between sharp and soft; right = rapid pitch changes sharpen the stroke, steady
            flight stays sinusoidal.
          %li
            <strong>Ferocity P</strong> (optional) from 5 — blends P authority into the dwell.
          %li
            <strong>Balance (I→Asymmetry) last, low.</strong> This is the most destabilizing
            channel; 5–15 only. It counters persistent drift, never transient bumps.
          %li
            <strong>SSFF</strong> from 5, only after cadence and ferocity hold. Too high =
            over-correction and half-stroke flutter; right = visible damping of pitch
            oscillation amplitude.
          %li
            <strong>Aeroelastic coefficients last.</strong> Tune glide first (wings still), then
            flap. Keep the gyro an authoritative copilot, not a nervous pilot.
        %aside.waveform-caution
          %strong= doc_caution_title
          = doc_caution_body

        %h2= st_h2_status
        %p
          <strong>Honest map, current firmware (Ornithopter.cpp @ 72c63020).</strong> The ideal
          laws above are the <em>design</em>; the code contains the full parameter surface, the
          constants, the WebUI sliders and the bridge shim — but a flight-test scaffolding
          block currently disconnects most of the gyro from the servo kernel:
        %pre
          %code
            :preserve
              // Ornithopter.cpp:269-275  (inside the flapping branch)
              #ifdef ZEPHYRUS_ENABLED
                  // STEP 7: ALL gyro values hard-zeroed (NaN guard test)
                  _osc.kGainMod = 1.0f;              // cadence channel OFF
                  gyroRudderCorrection  = 0.0f;      // crest rudder OFF while flapping
                  gyroAileronCorrection = 0.0f;
                  gyroElevatorCorrection= 0.0f;
              #endif
        %p
          Committed with the i18n work as a "STEP 7" NaN-guard test and never rolled back, this
          block means today's servo-kernel behaviour is:
        %ul
          %li
            <strong>Cadence, Ferocity-PD, Balance, Resonance, SSFF</strong> — all present as
            parameters and in the bridge, but their consumption sites
            (<code>kGainMod</code>, <code>ferocitySignal</code>, <code>iBias</code>,
            <code>resonanceBias</code>) are pinned to zero or reset every frame
            (Ornithopter.cpp:282–284, 311–313, 354).
          %li
            <strong>Crest rudder stabilization</strong> is zeroed <em>inside the flapping
            branch</em>, so the rudder correction currently acts only in the glide branch — the
            opposite of what flight needs (most disturbances arrive mid-flap).
          %li
            <strong>The gearbox kernel is the live exception.</strong> It reads
            <code>gyroAileronCorrection</code> / <code>gyroElevatorCorrection</code> /
            <code>gyroRudderCorrection</code> and applies them with clamps (lines 436–451) — the
            STEP-7 zeroing block lives only in the servo kernel.
        %p
          This is not a criticism of the math — it is a <em>known, named</em> state: the
          stabilization modules are compiled in, parameterized, bridged, and waiting for the
          STEP-7 scaffolding to be removed and replaced by a real NaN policy (the
          <code>Validatio</code> guards already give the MPU data a clean path). Until then,
          treat the Ondas/SSFF/resonance tuning guide above as the flight-ready specification,
          verified against Zephyrus.cpp:729–737 and OrnithopterWaveform.h, but not yet airborne.
        %p
          The authoritative design record remains
          <a href="https://github.com/dantiel/PteronautOS/blob/master/docs/STABILIZATION.md" target="_blank">STABILIZATION.md</a>;
          its sibling project
          <a href="https://dantiel.github.io/OrniFlight/" target="_blank">OrniFlight</a>
          holds the continuously advanced ONDAS research.
